// ── SKIN · learn ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
const SKIN_LEARN = [
  { id: "skin-barrier", ic: "🧱", title: "Building a healthy skin barrier",
    desc: "Almost every skin problem traces back here first.",
    body: ["The outermost layer of skin works like a brick wall — cells as the bricks, lipids like ceramides and cholesterol as the mortar. Its job is to keep water in and irritants out.",
      "Over-cleansing, over-exfoliating, hot water, harsh actives and cold weather all erode the mortar. Once it's compromised, water escapes and irritants get in, so skin becomes dry, reactive and inexplicably sensitive to products it used to tolerate.",
      "Repair is unglamorous: stop the aggravating things, use a bland ceramide moisturiser, and wait two to four weeks. There's no shortcut, and no serum accelerates it."],
    note: "If skincare has started stinging when it never used to, that's a damaged barrier, not sensitive skin you were born with." },
  { id: "vitamin-c", ic: "🍊", title: "Vitamin C explained",
    desc: "Why it's fussy, and whether the expensive one is worth it.",
    body: ["Vitamin C is an antioxidant that neutralises free radicals from UV and pollution, supports collagen production, and fades pigmentation over months.",
      "Pure L-ascorbic acid is the most studied form and the most unstable — it oxidises with light, air and heat, turning orange or brown. Once that happens it's done. Derivatives are more stable but less proven.",
      "Formulation matters more than concentration. The well-known 15–20% L-ascorbic acid with vitamin E and ferulic acid combination exists because those three together are more stable and effective than any alone."],
    note: "If your vitamin C has turned orange, it's oxidised. Keep it away from light and buy small bottles you'll finish." },
  { id: "retinol-safely", ic: "🌙", title: "Retinol without destroying your barrier",
    desc: "The reason most people quit is the reason it wasn't working.",
    body: ["Retinoids speed up how quickly skin cells turn over. That's why they work on texture, tone, fine lines and acne — and why they irritate.",
      "The standard mistake is starting nightly at a high strength. Skin gets red, peels and stings, so it gets abandoned around week three, right before the benefits would have appeared.",
      "Start twice a week. Pea-sized for the entire face. Moisturiser after — or sandwich it between two layers if you're sensitive. Add a night every few weeks as skin tolerates it. Reaching nightly use might take six months and that's completely fine."],
    note: "Not during pregnancy or breastfeeding. Azelaic acid is the pregnancy-safe swap and it's genuinely effective for tone and breakouts." },
  { id: "sunscreen-how", ic: "☀️", title: "How sunscreen actually works",
    desc: "Mineral versus chemical, and why SPF numbers mislead.",
    body: ["Mineral filters — zinc oxide, titanium dioxide — sit on skin and largely reflect and scatter UV. Chemical filters absorb UV and convert it to heat. Both work. Neither is more 'natural' in any meaningful sense.",
      "SPF measures UVB protection only, which is what burns you. UVA is what ages skin and passes through window glass — for that you want 'broad spectrum' or a high PA rating.",
      "The numbers are less different than they look: SPF 30 blocks around 97% of UVB, SPF 50 around 98%. Applying enough matters far more than the number on the bottle, and most people apply about a third of the tested amount."],
    note: "Makeup with SPF 15 doesn't count. You'd need roughly seven times the normal amount of foundation to reach the number on the label." },
  { id: "hormonal-acne", ic: "🌀", title: "Hormonal acne",
    desc: "Jawline, chin, cyclical — and why topicals alone often stall.",
    body: ["Hormonal acne typically appears along the jaw, chin and lower cheeks, flares in the week before a period, and tends toward deep tender bumps rather than surface whiteheads.",
      "It's driven by androgens increasing oil production and changing how skin cells shed. That's happening below the surface, which is why spot treatments applied on top so often disappoint.",
      "Topicals still help — adapalene and azelaic acid especially. But persistent hormonal acne frequently needs something systemic, and that's a conversation with a doctor rather than a shopping problem."],
    note: "Sudden adult acne alongside irregular periods, hair thinning or unusual hair growth is worth mentioning to your GP — PCOS is common and very treatable." },
  { id: "pregnancy-safe", ic: "🤰", title: "Pregnancy-safe skincare",
    desc: "What to pause, what's fine, and what actually replaces it.",
    body: ["Pause: retinoids of all kinds, including over-the-counter retinol and adapalene. High-dose salicylic acid taken orally. Hydroquinone.",
      "Generally considered fine: azelaic acid, glycolic and lactic acid, niacinamide, vitamin C, hyaluronic acid, benzoyl peroxide in normal topical amounts, and every mineral sunscreen.",
      "Azelaic acid is the most useful swap — it addresses tone, redness and breakouts, which covers most of what you'd miss from a retinoid.",
      "Melasma often appears in pregnancy and is strongly driven by UV. Daily sunscreen matters more now than at any other time."],
    note: "This is general information. Bring anything you're unsure about to your midwife or obstetrician — they'd far rather answer than have you guess." },
  { id: "collagen-myths", ic: "🧬", title: "Collagen myths",
    desc: "What the creams can and can't do.",
    body: ["Collagen molecules are far too large to penetrate the skin barrier. A cream containing collagen sits on the surface as a perfectly decent moisturiser — it does not deposit collagen where it's lost.",
      "What does have evidence for stimulating your own collagen: retinoids, sunscreen (by preventing the breakdown in the first place), and in-clinic procedures like microneedling and lasers.",
      "Oral collagen peptides are more interesting than the creams. Several trials show modest improvements in skin elasticity and hydration — the effect appears real but small, and the research is often industry-funded."],
    note: "If a product promises to 'replace lost collagen', it's describing something a topical cannot physically do." },
]


