const NAIL_LEARN = [
  { id: "gel-dip-acrylic", ic: "🔬", title: "Gel vs Dip vs Acrylic",
    desc: "Three different chemistries, three different trade-offs.",
    body: ["Gel is cured under UV or LED light. It's flexible, looks natural, and soaks off in acetone. Least damaging when removed properly.",
      "Dip powder is a resin plus a fine acrylic powder, applied in layers. It's very durable and needs no lamp, but the communal-jar method used in some salons is a genuine hygiene concern — ask for powder to be poured over your nail instead.",
      "Acrylic is a liquid monomer plus polymer powder that hardens in air. Strongest and best for length, but rigid, and removal requires longer acetone soaking or filing.",
      "None of them damage nails inherently. Removal is where nearly all the damage happens."],
    note: "If a salon files your natural nail plate aggressively before applying anything, find another salon. Prep should roughen the surface, not thin it." },
  { id: "gelx", ic: "💎", title: "Gel-X explained",
    desc: "The soft gel tip that largely replaced acrylic extensions.",
    body: ["Gel-X is a pre-shaped soft gel tip applied over the whole natural nail with gel adhesive and cured under a lamp. There's no filing of the natural nail and no dust or fumes.",
      "It's lighter than acrylic, more flexible, and soaks off in acetone in about twenty minutes rather than being filed away.",
      "The trade-off is strength: Gel-X doesn't hold extreme length as well as acrylic, and a badly sized tip lifts at the sidewalls."],
    note: "Correct tip sizing matters more than anything else. A tip that's slightly too narrow will lift within a week no matter how well it's applied." },
  { id: "builder-gel", ic: "🧱", title: "Builder gel explained",
    desc: "For growing your own nails out rather than replacing them.",
    body: ["Builder gel is a thicker viscosity gel applied as an overlay on the natural nail. It adds strength without adding much length, which makes it the usual recommendation for someone recovering from damage.",
      "Because it's an overlay, it grows out with the nail and can be infilled at the base rather than removed entirely.",
      "It comes in hard gel — which must be filed off — and soft or soak-off builder, which removes in acetone. Ask which you're getting."],
    note: "If your nails are thin and you want to grow them, builder gel is usually a better answer than a strengthener. It physically protects the nail while it grows." },
  { id: "nail-anatomy", ic: "🔎", title: "Nail anatomy",
    desc: "What each part does, and why it changes what you should do.",
    body: ["The nail plate is dead keratin — nothing applied to it can 'nourish' or repair it. It can only be protected, sealed or hydrated.",
      "The matrix, hidden under the skin at the base, is where the nail is actually made. Damage there is what causes permanent ridges or deformity.",
      "The lunula is the visible front edge of the matrix. The cuticle is the seal between skin and plate — its whole job is keeping bacteria out.",
      "Nails grow roughly 3mm a month on hands, slower on toes and slower in winter."],
    note: "A single dark stripe running the length of one nail that's new or changing should be seen by a doctor. Most are harmless, but this is the one nail change worth checking." },
  { id: "why-peel", ic: "📄", title: "Why nails peel",
    desc: "Almost always mechanical, almost never a vitamin problem.",
    body: ["The nail plate is made of layers. Peeling — onychoschizia — is those layers separating, and repeated wet-dry cycling is the leading cause.",
      "Filing back and forth with a coarse board, picking off polish, and acetone exposure all accelerate it.",
      "Genuine nutritional causes exist but are much rarer than supplement marketing suggests. Iron deficiency is the most common one worth checking, particularly if you're also tired or your periods are heavy."],
    note: "Before buying a supplement, try gloves, a glass file and nightly oil for eight weeks. That fixes most peeling on its own." },
  { id: "nail-growth-myths", ic: "🚫", title: "Nail growth myths",
    desc: "What doesn't work, so you can stop paying for it.",
    body: ["Biotin only helps if you're deficient, which is uncommon. It also interferes with thyroid and cardiac blood tests, which matters more than most people realise.",
      "Nothing applied topically makes nails grow faster — growth happens at the matrix, under the skin. Topicals can only reduce breakage, which makes nails appear to grow because you keep the length.",
      "Letting nails 'breathe' between manicures isn't a physiological thing — the nail plate is dead and receives nothing from the air. Breaks are useful because they let you see damage, not because nails need oxygen."],
    note: "Tell any clinician you take biotin before blood tests. It can skew thyroid and troponin results badly enough to cause real diagnostic confusion." },
]


