const LIP_LEARN = [
  { id: "why-chapped", ic: "🍂", title: "Why lips become chapped",
    desc: "The structural reason, and the habit that makes it worse.",
    body: ["Lip skin is three to five cell layers thick, against roughly sixteen elsewhere on the face. It has no oil glands and no sweat glands, so it cannot self-moisturise the way the rest of your skin does.",
      "That means lips lose water far faster than they can replace it. Cold air, wind, central heating, dehydration and mouth-breathing all accelerate it.",
      "Licking is the biggest self-inflicted cause. Saliva evaporates within seconds and takes surface moisture with it, and it contains digestive enzymes that irritate already-damaged skin. The relief lasts less than a minute and the damage compounds."],
    note: "Persistently cracked corners — angular cheilitis — are often fungal rather than dry, and won't clear with balm. Worth a GP visit if it lasts more than two weeks." },
  { id: "lip-spf-explained", ic: "☀️", title: "Lip SPF explained",
    desc: "Why the lower lip specifically, and what to look for.",
    body: ["Lips have very little melanin, so almost no built-in UV protection. The lower lip catches the most direct sun because of its angle, and it accounts for the large majority of lip skin cancers.",
      "Ordinary balms don't help — some are occlusive enough to feel protective while offering no UV filtering at all.",
      "Look for SPF 30 or higher and reapply after eating or drinking, which is far more frequent than on the rest of your face. Zinc-based formulas suit sensitive lips best."],
    note: "A rough, scaly patch on the lower lip that doesn't heal within a few weeks should be checked. Actinic cheilitis is precancerous and very treatable when caught early." },
  { id: "oils-vs-balms", ic: "⚖️", title: "Lip oils vs balms",
    desc: "They do different jobs, which is why you probably want both.",
    body: ["Balms are mostly occlusive — waxes and petrolatum that form a physical barrier to slow water loss. They seal in what's already there but add little themselves.",
      "Oils are emollient — they soften and smooth the surface, and are absorbed rather than sitting entirely on top. They feel lighter and are pleasant to reapply.",
      "The most effective routine uses both for what they're good at: oil through the day for comfort and colour, an occlusive balm at night when there's nothing to rub it off."],
    note: "If you're constantly reapplying balm and lips are no better, you likely have a barrier problem. Two weeks of plain petrolatum at night, no flavour, no exfoliation." },
  { id: "plumping-myths", ic: "🚫", title: "Lip plumping myths",
    desc: "How plumpers work, which is not how they're marketed.",
    body: ["Plumping glosses work by mild irritation. Cinnamon, capsicum, menthol and ginger cause local inflammation, which brings blood to the area and creates temporary swelling lasting perhaps thirty minutes.",
      "It's a controlled irritation, not a treatment. Used often, it can leave lips drier and more sensitive than they started.",
      "Hyaluronic acid in a topical lip product cannot travel deep enough to plump tissue. It hydrates the surface, which smooths lines and can make lips look marginally fuller — a real but modest effect.",
      "Anything that genuinely changes lip volume is a filler, and that's a medical procedure."],
    note: "If a lip product tingles or burns, that sensation is inflammation. Occasionally is fine; daily is not." },
  { id: "lip-finishes", ic: "✨", title: "Matte vs satin vs gloss",
    desc: "Which finish suits which lip, and why.",
    body: ["Matte contains more pigment and less emollient. It lasts longest, looks the most defined, and is the most drying — it clings to any flake or fine line.",
      "Satin is the middle and the most universally flattering. Enough emollient to stay comfortable, enough pigment to read as lipstick, and it reflects a little light so lips look fuller.",
      "Gloss reflects the most light, which reads as fullest, but transfers and moves most. Modern non-sticky glosses have largely solved the unpleasant part.",
      "The general rule: the drier or more mature the lip, the further from matte you want to be."],
    note: "If matte is the only finish you like, prep is everything — mask the night before, balm fifteen minutes ahead, blot, then apply." },
]


