const BODY_LEARN = [
  { id: "what-is-kp", ic: "🫧", title: "What causes KP?",
    desc: "The bumps a third of people have and most were never given a name for.",
    body: ["Keratosis pilaris happens when keratin — the protein your skin is made of — builds up and plugs the opening of a hair follicle. Each plug becomes a small rough bump, sometimes with a trapped hair curled inside, often with a ring of redness around it.",
      "It shows up most on the backs of the upper arms, the fronts of the thighs, the buttocks and sometimes the cheeks. It's more noticeable in winter, in dry climates, and in people with eczema, asthma or hay fever in the family.",
      "It affects roughly a third of people and is strongly hereditary. It is not caused by poor hygiene, not caused by diet, and not caused by anything you did. It very commonly improves on its own by your thirties or forties.",
      "What helps: chemical exfoliants that dissolve the plug — urea, lactic acid, salicylic acid — used consistently, followed by moisturiser on damp skin. What doesn't: scrubbing, which inflames the surrounding skin and makes the redness worse without touching the plug underneath."],
    note: "KP is a management routine, not a cure. If it clears and you stop treating, it comes back — that isn't the product failing, it's how the condition works." },
  { id: "glycolic-vs-salicylic", ic: "⚗️", title: "Glycolic acid vs salicylic acid",
    desc: "Water-soluble versus oil-soluble, and why that decides which you need.",
    body: ["Glycolic acid is an alpha hydroxy acid — water-soluble, so it works on the skin's surface, loosening the bonds between dead cells. Best for rough texture, dullness and uneven tone.",
      "Salicylic acid is a beta hydroxy acid — oil-soluble, so it can get inside the pore and clear it from within. Best for congestion, blackheads and body acne.",
      "For KP, either can help, and formulas often combine them. For body acne, salicylic is the clear choice. For rough or ashy texture, glycolic.",
      "Both increase sun sensitivity, and both are more irritating on freshly shaved skin."],
    note: "Don't layer several acids on body skin at once. One, consistently, for eight weeks beats three at once for two." },
  { id: "body-acne", ic: "🔴", title: "Body acne explained",
    desc: "Why it appears where it does, and what actually clears it.",
    body: ["Chest, back and shoulders have a high density of oil glands, which is why acne concentrates there. Add friction from clothing and straps, plus trapped sweat, and you have the conditions for it.",
      "Benzoyl peroxide wash is the most effective over-the-counter treatment. Leave it on for a full sixty seconds before rinsing — that contact time is what makes it work, and almost nobody does it. It will bleach towels and dark fabrics.",
      "Small, uniform, itchy bumps that don't respond to acne treatment may be fungal — malassezia folliculitis — which needs an antifungal instead. That's a common misdiagnosis worth knowing about.",
      "Shower promptly after sweating, and change out of damp gym clothes rather than sitting in them."],
    note: "If back acne is deep, painful and scarring, that's worth seeing a doctor for rather than working through the shelf. It's treatable, and scarring isn't reversible." },
  { id: "stretch-mark-myths", ic: "🌗", title: "Stretch mark myths",
    desc: "What the evidence actually shows, which is less than the marketing does.",
    body: ["Stretch marks are tears in the dermis caused by rapid stretching. They begin red or purple — striae rubrae — and mature to silvery white, striae albae.",
      "No cream has been shown to reliably prevent them. Genetics is the strongest predictor, followed by how fast the stretching happens. Cocoa butter and vitamin E, the two most marketed, have repeatedly failed to outperform placebo in trials.",
      "Once formed, red marks respond considerably better than white ones — tretinoin has some evidence in the red phase. White marks are mature scar tissue and topicals do very little.",
      "Treatments with genuine evidence are procedural: microneedling, fractional laser and radiofrequency. They improve appearance rather than remove."],
    note: "Around eight in ten women get stretch marks in pregnancy. They are a mark of what your body did, not evidence of anything you failed to do." },
  { id: "barrier-below-neck", ic: "🧱", title: "Your skin barrier below the neck",
    desc: "Same organ, same rules, far less attention.",
    body: ["Body skin has the same barrier structure as facial skin but fewer oil glands, particularly on the shins, forearms and back. It's also exposed to hotter water and harsher cleansers than you'd ever put on your face.",
      "Hot showers are the most common cause of body dryness and winter itch. They strip lipids faster than anything else in a normal routine.",
      "Fragranced body wash is the second. Fragrance is the most common cause of contact dermatitis, and body wash covers a very large surface area.",
      "The fix is unglamorous: warm rather than hot water, a gentle unfragranced wash, and cream on damp skin."],
    note: "Itchy shins in winter that you're tempted to scratch until they mark is almost always this, not a skin condition. Two weeks of warm showers and a proper cream usually settles it." },
]


